## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical, end-to-end protection of PII in a CNPA pipeline with minimal friction for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Field-level redaction policy
- Transformations across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis
- Validation and leak-coverage observability

## Code Example
```javascript
function redact(record, policy){
  if (policy.email) record.email = mask(record.email);
  if (policy.phone) record.phone = mask(record.phone);
  return record;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify policy changes against compliance requirements without disrupting traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes understanding of cache invalidation, versioned keys, outbox patterns, and end-to-end consistency in CNPA stacks. It also checks how to observe impact and plan safe rollouts.

## Key Concepts
- Cache invalidation strategies and TTLs
- Redis key versioning and cache-aside pattern
- Event-driven invalidation via Kafka
- Safe reads with read-fallbacks and refresh
- Observability of latency and hit ratios

## Code Example
```javascript
// Update path
BEGIN;
UPDATE profiles SET name=$1, email=$2, version = version + 1 WHERE id=$id;
COMMIT;
redis.del(`profile:${id}:v*`);
publishKafka('profile_updated', { id, version: NEW_VERSION });
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions when read happens concurrently with update?
- How would you tune TTLs and decide when to prune old keys?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of practical, beginner-friendly schema evolution across HTTP input, DB writes, and Kafka events without breaking existing consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Backward-compatible schema evolution
- Optional/additive fields and defaults
- Schema registry with compatibility levels
- Canary rollout and feature flags
- Contract and end-to-end testing

## Code Example

```javascript
const Ajv = require("ajv");
const ajv = new Ajv();
const schema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    id: { type: "string" },
    event: { type: "string" },
    customer_segment: { type: "string" }
  },
  required: ["id", "event"],
  additionalProperties: true
};
function validate(payload){
  const valid = ajv.validate(schema, payload);
  if(!valid) throw new Error(ajv.errors.map(e=>e.message).join(", "));
  return payload;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle a future breaking change if a client needs the new field immediately?
- How would you monitor and alert on schema compatibility errors in prod?

